/-!
# The ideal sheaf of an effective Cartier divisor is of finite type

`Stacks.Algebraization.idealKernel_isQuasicoherent` shows that the sheaf of ideals of a
quasi-coherent ideal sheaf datum — the kernel of `𝒪_X ⟶ (ι_I)_* 𝒪_{V(I)}` — is quasi-coherent.
The annihilator-subsheaf API of [Stacks, Tag 01PO] asks for one more property of that ideal
sheaf before it will conclude anything: that it is of **finite type**. This file supplies it for
the ideal sheaf of an effective Cartier divisor, which is the case every divisor-theoretic
consumer meets.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.idealKernel_isFiniteType_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor`: the sheaf of
  ideals of an effective Cartier divisor is of finite type.

## Implementation notes

Nothing here is new mathematics; the point is that the two halves of the argument are stated at
spellings that do not meet, and this file is the one place where they are joined.

`Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor I` is, by definition, invertibility of
`Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheaf I.subschemeι.toLRSHom.toShHom`, and
`Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isFiniteType_of_isInvertibleModule` already carries an invertible
module to one of finite type. So the mathematical content is entirely present; what is missing is
plumbing of two kinds.

**The invertibility is not *found*, only *equal*.** The predicate that
`isFiniteType_of_isInvertibleModule` consumes is `Stacks.Ample.IsInvertibleModule X L`, which
unfolds to `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` with the sheaf condition on the structure
presheaf supplied, whereas `IsEffectiveCartierDivisor` supplies that condition as
`(structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property`. The two propositions are definitionally equal —
the sheaf condition is a `Prop`, so proof irrelevance identifies the two witnesses — but
instance search is syntactic and does not see it. Hence the hypothesis is taken as an explicit
argument and re-offered through a `have` at the spelling the consumer names, rather than left to
`infer_instance`, which fails here.

**The ideal sheaf has two shapes.** `Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheaf` is the *categorical*
kernel, while the subsheaf-of-`𝒪_X` form that Tag 01PO consumes is
`Stacks.Algebraization.kernelSubmodule`, the sectionwise kernel. `kernelSubmoduleIso` identifies
them, and `AlgebraicGeometry.isFiniteType_of_iso` transports finite type across it. The
instance argument of `isFiniteType_of_isInvertibleModule` is given explicitly for the same
reason as above: at the `Limits.kernel` spelling the search would again fail.

Stating the result for `Stacks.Algebraization.kernelSubmodule` rather than for a divisor-specific
abbreviation is deliberate, and is what lets
`AlgebraicGeometry.DivisorSum.FittingFiltrations` apply it: `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSubsheaf` is
an `abbrev` for exactly this kernel submodule, so no further bridging is needed there.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01PO](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PO)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WR)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05P2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P2)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry

universe u

namespace Stacks.Algebraization

/-- **The sheaf of ideals of an effective Cartier divisor is of finite type.** Being an effective
Cartier divisor is invertibility of the ideal sheaf, an invertible module is finite locally free
of rank one, and such a module is generated by one section near every point.

The statement is about `Stacks.Algebraization.kernelSubmodule`, the sectionwise kernel of
`𝒪_X ⟶ (ι_I)_* 𝒪_{V(I)}`, which is the subsheaf-of-`𝒪_X` form that [Stacks, Tag 01PO] consumes;
it matches `Stacks.Algebraization.idealKernel_isQuasicoherent`, the other hypothesis that tag
places on an ideal sheaf. -/
theorem idealKernel_isFiniteType_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (I : X.IdealSheafData) (hI : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor I) :
    (kernelSubmodule (SheafOfModules.unitToPushforwardObjUnit
      I.subschemeι.toRingCatSheafHom)).toSheafOfModules.IsFiniteType :=
  have : Stacks.Ample.IsInvertibleModule X
    (Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheaf I.subschemeι.toLRSHom.toShHom) := hI
  AlgebraicGeometry.isFiniteType_of_iso (kernelSubmoduleIso _).symm
    (Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.isFiniteType_of_isInvertibleModule
      (Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheaf I.subschemeι.toLRSHom.toShHom))

end Stacks.Algebraization

end
